## Why this topic matters

Every extended response question in Paper 2 opens with a scenario about
an organisation. Before you can evaluate what digital tools that
organisation needs, whether a proposed system is feasible, or what
risks a project carries, you have to read the scenario and understand
what kind of organisation it describes.

Students who rush past this step lose marks on every question that
follows. A student who reads "a local health charity" as "a business"
will give a fundamentally wrong answer, no matter how much they know
about the technology involved.

## The two fundamental purposes

Every organisation exists to do one of two things, or both:

**Provide a product.** A product is a tangible good that transfers
ownership to a customer when purchased. A supermarket sells products.
A car manufacturer sells products. A software publisher that ships
boxed copies on a disc is selling a product.

**Provide a service.** A service is an action, experience, or
expertise that the customer receives without anything physical
changing hands. A hairdresser provides a service. A bank provides
a service. A streaming platform provides a service (even though it
uses software to do it, what the customer buys is ongoing access,
not the software itself).

Most organisations in practice provide both. A restaurant sells a
product (the food) wrapped in a service (the atmosphere, the waiting
staff, the table booking). A mobile phone network sells a product
(the handset) bundled with a service (the calls, data, and support).
The ability to see both elements and name them correctly is what the
exam rewards.

## The three sectors

Alongside purpose, every organisation belongs to a sector. The sector
describes who owns the organisation and who it exists to benefit.

**Private sector.** Privately owned, run for profit, answerable to
owners or shareholders. The large majority of British businesses sit
here: retailers, manufacturers, most technology companies, banks,
insurers.

**Public sector.** Owned by government, funded by taxation, run for
public benefit. The NHS, state schools, the BBC, local councils,
the armed forces. A public sector organisation may still charge
for some things (prescription charges, school lunches) but its
fundamental funding and purpose come from the state.

**Third sector.** Neither government-owned nor profit-driven.
Charities, voluntary organisations, community groups, social
enterprises. Third sector organisations exist to advance a cause
(medical research, poverty relief, arts access) and any surplus they
make is reinvested into that cause rather than paid to owners.

## Why purpose and sector together matter

These two questions, what the organisation does and whose benefit it
exists for, together shape every digital decision an organisation
makes:

- A private sector retailer buying e-commerce software cares about
  conversion rate and average order value, because every percentage
  point flows to the bottom line.

- A public sector hospital buying e-commerce-like software for patient
  appointments cares about accessibility, data protection, and
  integration with existing NHS systems, because its users are
  patients, not customers, and its obligations are statutory.

- A third sector charity buying donation software cares about trust,
  recurring giving, and Gift Aid handling, because its income depends
  on sustained relationships with supporters rather than one-off
  transactions.

The same underlying technology (a website that takes payments) carries
completely different requirements depending on the combination of
purpose and sector. Miss this in a Paper 2 answer and your evaluation
loses its grounding.

## What Section B expects

When a Paper 2 question gives you a scenario, you should:

1. Name the sector (private, public, third). The scenario usually
   tells you, but sometimes expects you to infer.
2. Name the purpose (product, service, or both). Again, sometimes
   explicit, sometimes inferred.
3. Use the combination to justify your analysis. "Because this is a
   charity..." or "Because the business sells products as well as
   offering a service..." anchors your evaluation in the specifics of
   the scenario, which is what AO3 marks reward.

The worked examples in the Method section demonstrate how to do this
in practice on past paper questions. Come back to those after you
have practiced the assessment below.
